<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.segal.syncstation</string>

  <!-- Sync worker via run-worker-foreground.sh, which EXECs the worker so this
       job process IS the worker. Foreground + KeepAlive → launchd keeps it alive
       and restarts it on crash or reboot (fixes the old reaping problem, where a
       nohup child died when the launcher script exited). The Cloud SQL proxy has
       its own agent (com.segal.sqlproxy); the wrapper waits for it. -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>/Users/marcelo/Projects/segal-case-tracker/scripts/run-worker-foreground.sh</string>
  </array>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    <key>HOME</key>
    <string>/Users/marcelo</string>
    <!-- Daily-sweep mode. The goal is to answer "which causas moved today",
         which needs the WHOLE portfolio visited often, not a drained PDF queue.

         DETAIL_PENDING_DOCS_FIRST is deliberately NOT set here. It ordered the
         rotation by "most pending PDFs first", i.e. it picked the most
         EXPENSIVE causas on every cycle: measured on this station, a causa with
         no new movements costs 1.1s while its PDFs cost 3.5s each. That
         ordering is a direct cause of the ~80-day full lap. Without it the
         rotation falls back to "least recently detailed first", which is what a
         sweep wants. Turn it back on only to drain a PDF backlog, and expect
         freshness to collapse while it is on.

         The larger batch and shorter cycle below are KEPT on purpose: the old
         defaults (30 / 4h) are slower, and a daily sweep needs more throughput,
         not less. Do not "restore the defaults" without re-reading this. -->
    <key>DETAIL_BATCH</key>
    <string>80</string>
    <key>SYNC_INTERVAL</key>
    <string>2</string>

    <!-- Email dispatch budget per sync, TEMPORARILY lowered from 25 while the
         rotation speeds up. The reordered rotation now visits causas unseen for
         ~30 days (or never), so each one dumps a month of backed-up movements at
         once: measured on 2026-10-02, the 25 ceiling was already being hit 14
         times in two days, and a single causa produced 38 notifications.
         Alerts are ALWAYS persisted and still show in the app's activity feed —
         this gates only the email/webhook send, so nothing is lost. Raise it
         back once the daily mail volume is known. -->
    <key>NOTIFY_MAX</key>
    <string>5</string>

    <!-- Historical PDFs downloaded per causa visit. Measured on 2026-10-05 over a
         118 min cycle: documents took 67 min (33 downloading 511 docs at 3.3s,
         34 waiting 511 x 4s DOCUMENT_INTER_DELAY), 57% of the clock, against 23
         min processing movements for 39 causas. The rotation now hits the most
         overdue causas and each brings ~13 documents, so the pace fell to ~20
         causas/hour versus ~440 baseline. With 3, PDFs of movements dated in the
         last 30 days (DOC_RECENT, default) are STILL all downloaded (last
         week's resolution); only the older ones are limited. Deferred docs stay pending and need
         another visit to the causa (their JWT lives 1 hour); the worker log
         line "document cap active" reports how many were deferred. 0 = no cap. -->
    <key>DOC_MAX</key>
    <string>3</string>

    <!-- false = esta estacion trae SOLO los PDFs frescos y saltea los historicos.
         Medido 2026-10-07: los PDFs se llevaban 45-52% del ciclo mientras PJUD
         corta el detalle a los ~60 min por cuenta, y 83% de la cola pendiente
         era de movimientos de mas de seis meses contra TRECE mas nuevos que una
         semana. El historico es trabajo de la estacion dedicada. Los diferidos
         quedan `pending`: no se pierde nada, se pospone. -->
    <key>DOC_HISTORICAL</key>
    <string>false</string>

    <!-- DELTA-SYNC. El estado diario del PJUD decide que causas reciben el
         detalle, en lugar de elegirlas por antiguedad.

         Medido: la demora entre que un movimiento ocurre en el PJUD y que lo
         vemos era de 17 dias de mediana (p99 99), porque una vuelta completa a
         la cartera tarda ~80 dias. El feed trae ~343 causas notificadas por dia
         (~25 por abogado) y dice exactamente cuales se movieron.

         El tope de 20 del lote de 30 NO es opcional: el 2026-10-15 el abogado
         17 recibio 190 notificaciones en un dia y el abogado 1 recibio 64. Sin
         tope, esas 190 se comerian seis lotes y congelarian su cola de causas
         olvidadas por dias. El piso de 10 (30 - 20) es lo unico que protege a
         las 2.150 causas nunca revisadas.

         DETAIL_FEED_MAX_PER_BATCH no se fija aca: su default ya es 20. Se
         agrega solo si hay que moverlo mirando datos reales.

         Requiere ESTADO_DIARIO_PROBE_ENABLED, que ya esta activo en el .env de
         esta estacion. Con el flag en false el comportamiento es byte a byte el
         de antes, asi que apagarlo es el camino de vuelta. -->
    <key>DETAIL_FEED_PRIORITY_ENABLED</key>
    <string>true</string>
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>15</integer>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/marcelo/Library/Logs/segal/worker.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/marcelo/Library/Logs/segal/worker.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
